1
00:05:23,333 --> 00:05:29,333
Nothing on earth could come between them.

2
00:10:46,666 --> 00:10:52,666
Titanic is a drama, romance film directed by James Cameron.

3
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:16,000
101-year-old Rose DeWitt Bukater tells the story of her
life aboard the Titanic, 84 years later.

4
00:21:33,333 --> 00:21:39,333
A young Rose boards the ship with her mother and fiancé.

5
00:26:56,666 --> 00:27:02,666
Meanwhile, Jack Dawson and Fabrizio De Rossi win
third-class tickets aboard the ship.

6
00:32:20,000 --> 00:32:26,000
Rose tells the whole story from Titanic's departure through to its
death—on its first and last voyage—on April 15, 1912.

7
00:37:43,333 --> 00:37:49,333
Jack Dawson, played by Leonardo DiCaprio.

8
00:43:06,666 --> 00:43:12,666
Rose DeWitt Bukater, played by Kate Winslet.

9
00:48:30,000 --> 00:48:36,000
Cal Hockley, played by Billy Zane.

10
00:53:53,333 --> 00:53:59,333
Molly Brown, played by Kathy Bates.

11
00:59:16,666 --> 00:59:22,666
Ruth DeWitt Bukater, played by Frances Fisher.

12
01:04:40,000 --> 01:04:46,000
Old Rose, played by Gloria Stuart.

13
01:10:03,333 --> 01:10:09,333
Titanic is a 1997 American epic historical romance film
written and directed by James Cameron.

14
01:15:26,666 --> 01:15:32,666
Incorporating both historical and fictional aspects, it is based
on accounts of the sinking of RMS Titanic in 1912.

15
01:20:50,000 --> 01:20:56,000
Leonardo DiCaprio and Kate Winslet star as members of different social classes
who fall in love during the ship's ill-fated maiden voyage.

16
01:26:13,333 --> 01:26:19,333
The ensemble cast includes Billy Zane, Kathy Bates, Frances Fisher, Bernard
Hill, Jonathan Hyde, Danny Nucci, David Warner and Bill Paxton.

17
01:31:36,666 --> 01:31:42,666
Cameron's inspiration came from his fascination with shipwrecks.

18
01:37:00,000 --> 01:37:06,000
He felt a love story interspersed with human loss would be
essential to convey the emotional impact of the disaster.

19
01:42:23,333 --> 01:42:29,333
Production began on September 1, 1995, when Cameron
shot footage of the Titanic wreck.

20
01:47:46,666 --> 01:47:52,666
The modern scenes on the research vessel were shot on board the Akademik Mstislav
Keldysh, which Cameron had used as a base when filming the wreck.

21
01:53:10,000 --> 01:53:16,000
Scale models, computer-generated imagery (CGI), and a reconstruction of the
Titanic built at Baja Studios were used to recreate the sinking.

22
01:58:33,333 --> 01:58:39,333
Titanic was initially in development at 20th Century Fox, but delays and a mounting
budget resulted in Fox partnering with Paramount Pictures for financial help.

23
02:03:56,666 --> 02:04:02,666
It was the most expensive film ever made at the time,
with a production budget of $200 million.

24
02:09:20,000 --> 02:09:26,000
Filming took place from July 1996 to March 1997.

25
02:14:43,333 --> 02:14:49,333
Titanic premiered at the Tokyo International Film Festival on November 1,
1997, and was released in the United States on December 19.

26
02:20:06,666 --> 02:20:12,666
It was distributed by Paramount Pictures in the United States and
Canada and by 20th Century Fox in other territories.

27
02:25:30,000 --> 02:25:36,000
It was praised for its visual effects, performances (particularly those of DiCaprio, Winslet, and Gloria
Stuart), production values, direction, score, cinematography, story, and emotional depth.

28
02:30:53,333 --> 02:30:59,333
Among other awards, the film received fourteen nominations at the 70th Academy
Awards and won eleven, including Best Picture and Best Director.

29
02:36:16,666 --> 02:36:22,666
In doing so, it tied both All About Eve (1950) for the record for the most Academy Award nominations, and Ben-Hur (1959) for the most Academy Awards won by a film, making Titanic the most successful individual film in
Academy Award history (these records would be matched by 2016's La La Land and 2003's The Lord of the Rings: The Return of the King respectively, with the nomination record surpassed by Sinners in 2026).

30
02:41:40,000 --> 02:41:46,000
With an initial worldwide gross of over $1.84 billion, becoming the highest-grossing film of 1997, Titanic was the first film to reach the billion-dollar mark (1993's Jurassic Park would
later become the earliest film to achieve the feat via re-releases), and was the highest-grossing film of all time until Cameron's next film, Avatar (2009), surpassed it in 2010.

31
02:47:03,333 --> 02:47:09,333
Income from the initial theatrical release, retail video, and
soundtrack sales and US broadcast rights exceeded $3.2 billion.

32
02:52:26,666 --> 02:52:32,666
Releases pushed the worldwide theatrical total to $2.264 billion, making Titanic the second film to gross more
than $2 billion worldwide after Avatar; as of 2023, it is the fourth-highest grossing film.

33
02:57:50,000 --> 02:57:56,000
In 2017, the Library of Congress selected it for preservation in the United States National
Film Registry as "culturally, historically, or aesthetically significant".

34
03:03:13,333 --> 03:03:19,333
The film explores themes of ship, soulmates, panic, shipwreck, atlantic ocean.

35
03:08:36,666 --> 03:08:42,666
The tone is emotional.
